# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1 Introduction
H1('1. Introduction')
P("Landslides kill thousands of people every year and repeatedly cut roads, farmland and settlements in mountain regions. A global database of non-seismically triggered fatal landslides recorded 55,997 deaths in 4862 events between 2004 and 2016, most of them in Asia [@froude2018], and earlier work showed that multi-hazard loss databases undercount landslide deaths by a wide margin [@petley2012]. Maps that show where future slope failures are most likely to start are therefore a basic input to land-use planning, road design and monitoring [@fell2008].")
P("Nepal combines steep, deeply weathered slopes in structurally complex Lesser Himalayan rocks with intense monsoon rainfall and extensive hillslope modification [@dhital2015]. Monsoon rainfall is the dominant trigger [@dahal2008], earthquakes add an episodic second trigger, as the 2015 Gorkha earthquake showed [@roback2018], and a recent national assessment placed much of the population exposed to rainfall-triggered landslides in the Middle Hills [@kincey2024]. Kavrepalanchowk District, east of Kathmandu, lies in this belt. Between 26 and 30 September 2024, an extreme late-monsoon rainstorm over central Nepal triggered widespread floods and landslides [@dhm2024], and Kavrepalanchowk was among the hardest-hit districts; the slope failures of that event provide the inventory used here.")
P("Landslide susceptibility is the spatial probability that a landslide occurs in an area given local terrain conditions; it says where failures are likely, not when or how large they will be [@varnes1984; @guzzetti1999]. Statistical and machine-learning (ML) classifiers now dominate the field [@reichenbach2018; @merghadi2020]. Tree ensembles such as random forest (RF) [@breiman2001; @catani2013; @trigila2015] and gradient boosting [@friedman2001; @chen2016; @sahin2020], together with support vector machines (SVM) [@cortes1995; @pourghasemi2018], are among the most frequently compared algorithms, including in recent remote-sensing studies [@jiang2023; @yu2023]. Comparative studies rarely find one algorithm that wins everywhere [@goetz2015; @pourghasemi2018], which is one argument for ensembles of diverse learners [@dietterich2000].")
P("The difficulty is less the choice of algorithm than the way performance is estimated and the way a model trained on points is transferred to a raster. Three problems recur. First, conditioning factors and inventories are spatially autocorrelated, so random train–test splits place near-duplicate terrain on both sides of the split and inflate accuracy [@brenning2005; @roberts2017; @ploton2020]; spatial cross-validation reduces this optimism [@valavi2019], and it has to be nested when hyperparameters are tuned [@schratz2019]. A recent comparison in this journal found random cross-validation overestimated landslide-model performance by 6–18% relative to spatial cross-validation [@kumar2025]. Second, landslide and non-landslide samples are seldom drawn by the same process; positional error and sampling design leave signatures that a flexible learner can exploit without learning anything about slope stability [@hussin2016; @steger2016a; @steger2016b]. Third, the model is applied to every pixel of a district, including environmental conditions absent from the training data. The area of applicability (AOA) concept quantifies where a spatial model is extrapolating [@meyer2021], but training–raster consistency is rarely checked in susceptibility studies. A related issue concerns probability calibration: balanced 50:50 samples and algorithm-specific score scales mean that raw classifier outputs are not probabilities of failure [@niculescu2005; @vancalster2019].")
P("This study addresses these problems for Kavrepalanchowk District. Our aim was not to propose a new classifier but to test how much of a typical ML susceptibility workflow survives a strict validation and deployment audit. The objectives were to (i) audit the training table against the raster stack used for mapping, including categorical legends, distribution shifts and multicollinearity; (ii) quantify spatial autocorrelation and geographic sampling bias and choose a spatial blocking scheme accordingly; (iii) estimate the discrimination and calibration of RF, XGBoost, gradient boosting (GBM), an SVM and three ensembles by nested spatial cross-validation, with every data-dependent choice confined to training folds; (iv) test whether satellite spectral indices act as conditioning factors or as a leakage channel; and (v) map relative susceptibility for the valid analysis domain together with inter-model dispersion, agreement and applicability layers whose relation to error is measured rather than assumed.")

