H1('2. Study Area')
P(f"Kavrepalanchowk District lies in Bagmati Province, central Nepal, between about 27.33° and 27.76° N and 85.39° and 85.88° E (Figure 1). The official boundary encloses about 1394 km² (1393.7 km² after projection to WGS 84 / UTM zone 45N, EPSG:32645). Elevation in the analysis grid ranges from 290 to 2928 m and slope from 0° to 75°. The district spans the Middle Hills and the Mahabharat Range and is underlain mainly by low- to medium-grade Lesser Himalayan metasediments (phyllite, schist, quartzite and dolomite) cut by major thrusts [@dhital2015]; geological and soil information for the district was taken from Survey Department of Nepal documents.")
P(f"Most annual rainfall falls during the June–September monsoon [@dahal2008]. In 2024, the district-average CHIRPS v2.0 rainfall was about 1297 mm, of which 1135 mm (88%) fell between June and September; July was the wettest month (359 mm). The June–September total ranged from about 730 to 1375 mm across the district, with the lowest totals in the central valleys. The rainfall that triggered the inventoried failures fell at the end of this period, on 26–30 September 2024 [@dhm2024]. Land use, as mapped by the Esri 10-m Sentinel-2 land use/land cover product [@karra2021], is a mosaic of forest, terraced agriculture and growing settlements, and rural road construction has cut many hillslopes [@kincey2024].")
P("The inventory used here is an event-focused record of the slope failures caused by the 26–30 September 2024 rainstorm. It is not an operational near-real-time product; the analysis was carried out after the event.")
FIG('figs/Fig1_study_area.png' if os.path.exists('figs/Fig1_study_area.png') else 'figs/Fig_samples_folds.png',
    '**Figure 1.** ' + ('Study area. (a) Location of Kavrepalanchowk District (red) in Nepal; (b) hillshaded elevation (30-m DEM) with rivers, main roads (OpenStreetMap), the district boundary and the 600 landslide and 600 non-landslide points. WGS 84 / UTM zone 45N.' if os.path.exists('figs/Fig1_study_area.png') else HL('Placeholder until the boundary and DEM rasters are processed: sample locations with 10-km validation blocks and the fold assignment of the first repeat (WGS 84 / UTM 45N). Final version: (a) Nepal, (b) Bagmati Province, (c) hillshade with rivers, roads and samples.')), 13.5)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3 Methods
H1('3. Materials and Methods')
P("Figure 2 summarizes the workflow. Its central rule is that no decision that depends on the data (predictor selection, transformation, hyperparameters, calibration, ensemble weights or choice of the best model) may use the observations on which performance is reported.")
FIG('figs/Fig_workflow.png', '**Figure 2.** Workflow. The outer loop of the nested spatial cross-validation provides all reported performance; the inner loop makes every data-dependent modelling decision. The pipeline refit on all samples is used only for mapping.', 15.0)

H2('3.1. Landslide and Non-Landslide Samples')
P(f"The inventory contains 600 landslide points and 600 non-landslide points, all labelled with the 2024 event year; one landslide point falls on a NoData pixel at the raster edge, so raster-based analyses use {V['n']} points. Coordinates were supplied in WGS 84 geographic coordinates and projected to EPSG:32645. No two points share a location. The median distance from a landslide point to its nearest landslide neighbour is {V['ll_med_m']:.0f} m (minimum {V['ll_min_m']:.0f} m), and only {V['ll_lt60']} pairs of landslide points lie within 60 m of each other, so we treat each landslide point as a separate failure. Both classes were digitized manually in Google Earth Engine by comparing two Sentinel image composites: a pre-event composite averaging the three months before 26 September 2024 and a post-event composite averaging the three months after it. A landslide point marks a new slope failure, visible on the post-event composite but absent from the pre-event one, with one point per failure.")
P(f"Non-landslide points were digitized manually on the same image pairs, on ground that showed no slope failure in either image. They lie a median {V['ls_nonls_med_km']:.2f} km from the nearest landslide point (10th percentile {V['ls_nonls_p10_m']:.0f} m; minimum {V['ls_nonls_min_m']:.0f} m). Because they were placed by an interpreter rather than drawn by a random design, they are not spread evenly across the district; this is the source of the location signal examined in Sections 4.2 and 4.10. A balanced 1:1 design was kept because it is the design of the existing inventory [@hussin2016]. Balance changes the base rate seen by the classifiers, so scores from models trained on these data are relative susceptibility scores rather than probabilities of failure (Section 3.6).")

H2('3.2. Conditioning Factors and Remote-Sensing Inputs')
P(f"Twenty-one candidate predictors were compiled (Table 1). Terrain variables were derived from the 1-arc-second (≈30 m) Shuttle Radar Topography Mission DEM (SRTM) [@farr2007]: elevation, slope, aspect, topographic position index (TPI) [@weiss2001], roughness, terrain ruggedness index (TRI), flow accumulation, curvature and topographic wetness index (TWI) [@beven1979; @moore1991]. Soil variables were organic carbon content and sand, silt and clay fractions, plus a categorical parent-soil class; they were digitized from official Survey Department of Nepal soil documents and converted to rasters with Python scripts in Google Colab. Rainfall was the cumulative monsoon total. Three spectral indices were computed in Google Earth Engine from Sentinel imagery: the enhanced vegetation index (EVI) [@huete2002], the modified normalized difference water index (MNDWI) [@xu2006] and the modified bare soil index (MBI) [@nguyen2021]. Euclidean distances to the nearest building, river and road completed the set. Aspect is circular, so it was replaced by northness (cos aspect) and eastness (sin aspect), both set to zero on flat cells (slope = 0°). Curvature was dropped from the final models because the supplied curvature raster is an exact linear rescaling of the TPI raster (Section 4.1).")
TAB('**Table 1.** Candidate predictors, data sources and treatment.',
    ['Predictor', 'Group', 'Source / product (native resolution)', 'Date or period', 'Treatment in final models'],
    [['Elevation, slope, aspect, TPI, roughness, TRI, curvature', 'Terrain', 'SRTM 1-arc-second DEM (≈30 m) [@farr2007]', 'February 2000 acquisition', 'Aspect → northness, eastness; curvature removed (= −TPI/100)'],
     ['Flow accumulation, TWI', 'Hydrology', 'SRTM DEM; computed in Google Earth Engine', '—', 'Yeo–Johnson if |skew| > 0.5'],
     ['Organic carbon, sand, silt, clay', 'Soil', 'Survey Department of Nepal soil documents, digitized (Google Colab)', '—', 'Continuous'],
     ['Parent soil (codes 1–5)', 'Soil', 'Survey Department of Nepal soil documents; 5-class legend', '—', 'One-hot; 4 points NoData'],
     ['Monsoon cumulative rainfall', 'Climate', 'CHIRPS v2.0 monthly (0.05°, ≈5.5 km); June–September sum', 'Jun–Sep 2024', 'Continuous' if RAIN else 'Not used (no valid raster; see Section 3.3)'],
     ['EVI, MNDWI, MBI', 'Spectral', 'Sentinel composites, Google Earth Engine', 'September 2024 (overlaps the event)', 'Model S (leakage test) only'],
     ['Distance to road, river, building', 'Proximity', 'Roads: OpenStreetMap; rivers: Survey Department of Nepal', '2026 extracts', 'Euclidean distance on 30-m grid (this study)'],
     ['Building footprints', 'Proximity', 'Google Open Buildings v3 [@sirko2021]', '2026 export', 'Rasterized for distance'],
     ['District boundary', 'Mask', 'Survey Department of Nepal administrative boundary (Nepal MUTM 84°E, Everest 1830), reprojected', '—', 'Analysis mask'],
     ['Landslide and non-landslide points', 'Labels', 'Manual digitization on 3-month pre- and post-event Sentinel composites in Google Earth Engine (this study)', 'Event 26–30 Sep 2024', 'Labels only']],
    'Abbreviations: TPI, topographic position index; TRI, terrain ruggedness index; TWI, topographic wetness index; EVI, enhanced vegetation index; MNDWI, modified normalized difference water index; MBI, modified bare soil index. All rasters were aligned to the 30-m elevation grid (EPSG:32645); continuous layers were resampled bilinearly and categorical layers by nearest neighbour. Resampling a coarse product to 30 m does not add 30-m information, and the effective scale of rainfall and soil predictors is that of their native grids.')
P(f"The spectral indices need particular care. If the imagery post-dates the landslides, bare scars raise MBI and lower EVI at landslide points, and a classifier can then detect failures that have already happened rather than predict susceptibility. The imagery used for the spectral indices dates from September 2024 and overlaps the 26–30 September event and its aftermath. We therefore fitted two predictor sets: **Model T**, with terrain, hydrology, soil{', rainfall' if RAIN else ''} and proximity predictors, and **Model S**, which adds the three spectral indices. Model T is the susceptibility model; Model S is reported only as a leakage test, to show how much a post-event index can inflate apparent skill.")

H2('3.3. Training–Raster Consistency Audit')
P("A susceptibility map is only as valid as the match between the predictor values a model was trained on and the raster values it is later applied to. We therefore audited three things before modelling. (1) Categorical legends: the set of class codes in the training table was compared with the set in the district raster. (2) Continuous distributions: the minimum, maximum and mean of each sample variable were compared with the same statistics over all valid raster pixels, and the share of sample values falling outside the raster range was recorded; a sample value outside the raster range cannot occur if samples were extracted from that raster. (3) Multicollinearity: variance inflation factors (VIF) were computed from ordinary least-squares regressions that include an intercept, because VIF computed without an intercept on uncentred, strictly positive variables is inflated by the variable means rather than by collinearity [@obrien2007; @dormann2013].")
P(f"For deployment, the training table must be rebuilt by sampling every predictor from the final aligned raster stack at the inventory coordinates, so that units, transformations and legends are identical in training and mapping. {('This was done for every Model T predictor: values were sampled at the nearest 30-m pixel of the aligned stack, and the training table used for all Model T results was rebuilt from these values (' + str(V['n']) + ' samples; one point fell on a NoData edge pixel).' + (' The rainfall raster was rebuilt from CHIRPS v2.0 monthly totals (June–September 2024 sum); cells outside the clipped export were filled from the nearest valid cell before bilinear resampling to 30 m.' if RAIN else ' No valid rainfall raster was available, so rainfall was left out of Model T.')) if RX else HL('Pending: rerun the pipeline on values extracted from the raster stack.')} Pixel-level extrapolation was assessed with the dissimilarity index (DI) and AOA of Meyer and Pebesma [@meyer2021], using standardized predictors weighted by permutation importance and a threshold derived from the cross-validated DI of the training data.")

H2('3.4. Spatial Structure and Choice of Validation Blocks')
P("Spatial dependence was described with Moran's I [@moran1950] for the landslide label and eight predictors in ten distance classes from 0–0.5 km to 15–20 km, using binary distance-band weights. To choose the size of the validation blocks empirically, we compared random 5-fold cross-validation, square blocks of 2, 5, 10 and 15 km assigned to five folds by stratified group k-fold, and k-means clusters of the coordinates (k = 10 and 20). Each scheme was repeated five times with random block-grid offsets, using default RF and XGBoost models. For each scheme we also fitted a coordinates-only model (easting and northing as the only inputs). A coordinates-only model cannot learn slope processes; if it scores well, the classes are separable by location alone and the validation scheme rewards that separation [@ploton2020].")

H2('3.5. Nested Spatial Cross-Validation and Models')
P(f"Performance was estimated by nested spatial cross-validation [@schratz2019]. The outer loop split the samples into five folds of 10-km blocks ({V['n_blocks10']} occupied blocks) with stratified group k-fold, repeated three times with different random block-grid offsets. Within each outer-training set, a 4-fold inner loop, again grouped by 10-km blocks, made every modelling decision: (1) the predictor set, chosen among all predictors, all predictors without parent soil, and a VIF-screened set (iterative removal of the highest VIF above 10, recomputed on the outer-training data), by the mean inner AUC of default RF and XGBoost models; (2) a Yeo–Johnson transformation [@yeo2000] of continuous predictors whose training-fold skewness exceeded 0.5 in absolute value, followed by standardization, both fitted on training data only and carried in the same pipeline object; (3) hyperparameters, by randomized search (20 draws, ROC AUC) [@bergstra2012] over the spaces in Table 2; and (4) calibration and ensemble weights (Section 3.6). The outer-test fold was used only once, to score the locked pipeline.")
PRt = T['PR']
def _mode(mn):
    q = PRt[PRt.model == mn].drop(columns=['rep', 'fold', 'model']).dropna(axis=1, how='all')
    out = []
    for c in q:
        v = q[c].astype(str).value_counts().index[0]
        v = v[:-2] if v.endswith('.0') else v
        out.append(f"{c}={v}")
    return '; '.join(out)
def _final(mn):
    if not fin:
        return '—'
    return '; '.join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in fin['params'][mn].items())
TAB('**Table 2.** Classifiers, hyperparameter search spaces, the most frequent selection across the 15 outer folds of Model T, and the configuration of the final mapping models. All per-fold selections are listed in Supplementary Table S3.',
    ['Model', 'Search space', 'Most frequent selection (outer folds)', 'Final mapping model'],
    [['RF [@breiman2001] (scikit-learn)', 'n_estimators {200, 300, 500}; max_depth {None, 5, 10, 15, 20}; min_samples_split {2, 5, 10}; min_samples_leaf {1, 2, 4}; max_features {sqrt, log2, 0.5}', _mode('RF'), _final('RF')],
     ['XGBoost [@chen2016] (hist)', 'n_estimators {100, 200, 300, 500}; max_depth {2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8}; learning_rate {0.01, 0.03, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2}; subsample {0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0}; colsample_bytree {0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 1.0}; min_child_weight {1, 3, 5, 10}; reg_lambda {0.5, 1, 5, 10}', _mode('XGBoost'), _final('XGBoost')],
     ['GBM [@friedman2001] (scikit-learn)', 'n_estimators {100, 200, 300}; max_depth {2, 3, 4, 5}; learning_rate {0.01, 0.03, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2}; subsample {0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0}; min_samples_leaf {1, 5, 10}', _mode('GBM'), _final('GBM')],
     ['SVM [@cortes1995] (scikit-learn SVC, RBF)', 'C {0.1, 0.3, 1, 3, 10, 30, 100}; gamma {scale, 0.003, 0.01, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3}', _mode('SVM'), _final('SVM')]],
    'Random search drew 20 configurations per model and outer fold (40 for the final mapping models), scored by spatial inner-fold ROC AUC.')

H2('3.6. Calibration and Ensembles')
P("Raw scores were the predicted class-1 probability for the tree models and a logistic transform of the decision function for the SVM. Within each outer-training set, inner out-of-fold scores were used to fit a Platt (logistic) recalibration for every model [@platt1999; @niculescu2005]; the fitted calibrator was then applied to the outer-test scores. Calibration targets the 50:50 base rate of the sample, so a calibrated score of 0.5 means even odds under the sampling design, not a 50% chance of failure in a pixel.")
P("Four combinations were compared with the single models, all built from inner out-of-fold scores only: an equal-weight mean of the four calibrated scores; a mean weighted by inner AUC minus 0.5; a logistic-regression stacker on the logit of the four raw scores; and the single model with the highest inner AUC (\"best single\"). The last option tests whether picking a winner inside the training data generalizes.")

H2('3.7. Performance Metrics and Statistical Comparison')
P("Outer-fold predictions were pooled within each repeat (each sample predicted once per repeat) and summarized as the mean ± standard deviation over the three repeats. Discrimination was measured by the area under the ROC curve (AUC) [@fawcett2006] and the area under the precision–recall curve (PR-AUC); threshold metrics at 0.5 on calibrated scores were accuracy, balanced accuracy, F₁ and the Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC); probabilistic accuracy by the Brier score and log loss; and calibration by the calibration slope, calibration-in-the-large (intercept with slope fixed at one) and the expected calibration error over ten bins [@vancalster2019]. Fold-level AUCs show the variation among geographic regions. Pairwise AUC differences on the pooled out-of-fold predictions were tested with DeLong's test [@delong1988] in the fast formulation of Sun and Xu [@sun2014], with 95% confidence intervals for each AUC and difference and Holm correction for the thirteen comparisons within each repeat [@holm1979]. Precision depends on prevalence; on the balanced sample it does not transfer to the much lower landslide prevalence of the landscape.")

H2('3.8. Predictor Importance')
P("Impurity-based importance is biased toward variables with many split points and is unstable under collinearity [@strobl2007]. We therefore used permutation importance computed on each outer-test fold: the drop in AUC when a predictor, or a whole predictor family, was randomly permuted (five permutations per fold). Families (terrain, hydrology, soil texture, parent soil, rainfall, spectral indices, proximity) were permuted jointly so that correlated substitutes could not mask each other.")

H2('3.9. Mapping, Zonation and Reliability Layers')
P(f"For mapping, the locked pipeline of each model was refit on all {V['n']} samples: the predictor set was the one chosen most often in the outer folds, hyperparameters were tuned by 5-fold 10-km-block cross-validation (40 random draws), and Platt calibrators were fitted to the resulting spatial out-of-fold scores. This refit produces no performance estimate. Raster inference applied the persisted transformation, scaling, model and calibrator to every valid pixel in batches. The primary output is the continuous calibrated ensemble score. A secondary communication layer divides the score into quintiles (Very Low to Very High), which are relative rank classes that hold 20% of the valid area each by construction. Each class is validated by the share and density of held-out landslide points it captures: in every outer fold, the fold's own pipeline was applied to the raster, quintile breaks were computed from that fold's map, and the held-out landslide points were assigned to classes.")
P("Two reliability layers accompany the score. Inter-model dispersion is the pixel-wise standard deviation of the four calibrated scores. It describes disagreement among algorithms only; it does not include inventory error, pseudo-absence uncertainty, measurement error or bias shared by all models. Model agreement is the number of models (0–4) whose calibrated score is at least 0.5, reported separately as unanimous non-susceptible (0 of 4) and unanimous susceptible (4 of 4) agreement. Before mapping these layers, we measured how out-of-fold error varies with agreement and dispersion on the samples, so that their meaning is empirical. The AOA mask (Section 3.3) marks pixels where the model extrapolates beyond the training data.")

H2('3.10. Software and Reproducibility')
vv = V['versions']
P(f"All analyses were run in Python {vv['python']} with scikit-learn {vv['sklearn']} [@pedregosa2011], XGBoost {vv['xgboost']}, NumPy {vv['numpy']}, pandas {vv['pandas']}, SciPy {vv['scipy']}, statsmodels {vv['statsmodels']}, pyproj and rasterio. The random seed was 42 for all estimators and fold generators; block-grid offsets for repeat r were drawn with seed r. The scripts, block and fold assignments, per-fold decisions and hyperparameters, and the environment file are provided as Supplementary Materials and in the repository listed in the Data Availability Statement.")

