P(f"The coordinates-only baseline exposed strong geographic structure in the inventory (Figure 4). Under random 5-fold cross-validation, an RF fitted to easting and northing alone reached an AUC of {f3(g('Random 5-fold','Coordinates only','RF (default)')[0])}. The score fell to {f3(g('Block 2km','Coordinates only','RF (default)')[0])} with 2-km blocks, {f3(g('Block 5km','Coordinates only','RF (default)')[0])} with 5-km blocks and {f3(g('Block 10km','Coordinates only','RF (default)')[0])} ± {f3(g('Block 10km','Coordinates only','RF (default)')[1])} with 10-km blocks. With the Model T predictors, RF fell much less, from {f3(g('Random 5-fold','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0])} (random) to {f3(g('Block 10km','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0])} (10-km blocks), and stayed at or above {f3(min(g(s,'Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0] for s in ['Block 15km','K-means 10','K-means 20']))} for the largest blocks and clusters. The 10-km scheme removes most of the location signal, matches the decorrelation distance of the label and keeps the median distance between a test point and the nearest training point at {V['sch'][V['sch'].scheme=='Block 10km'].nn.iloc[0]/1000:.1f} km, against {V['sch'][V['sch'].scheme=='Random 5-fold'].nn.iloc[0]/1000:.1f} km for random folds. It was adopted for all further validation. Larger blocks and coarser clusters lowered the predictor-model AUC further (to {f3(min(g(s_,'Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0] for s_ in ['Block 15km','K-means 10','K-means 20']))}) but left fewer occupied groups per fold and larger fold-to-fold variation; this further decline is consistent with the distance decay shown below.")
