# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5 Discussion
H1('5. Discussion')
H2('5.1. Spatial Validation and Sampling Bias')
P(f"Spatial blocking lowered the estimates for Model T: default RF fell from {f3(g('Random 5-fold','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0])} under random folds to {f3(g('Block 10km','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0])} with 10-km blocks and to {f3(g('K-means 10','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0])} with ten k-means clusters, a relative drop of {(1-g('Block 10km','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0]/g('Random 5-fold','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0])*100:.0f}–{(1-g('K-means 10','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0]/g('Random 5-fold','Model T predictors','RF (default)')[0])*100:.0f}%, comparable to the lower part of the 6–18% inflation reported by Kumar et al. [@kumar2025]. The tuned, calibrated ensemble reached {f3(m('Ensemble-equal','cal','AUC'))}. These estimates hold for the blocking scheme tested here; spatial cross-validation is not a substitute for a probability sample of the map [@wadoux2021]. The coordinates-only baseline is more revealing than the headline AUC. A model that knows nothing but location separated the classes with an AUC of {f2(g('Random 5-fold','Coordinates only','RF (default)')[0])} under random folds because the manually digitized non-landslide points sit, on average, {V['ls_nonls_med_km']:.1f} km from the nearest landslide point and occupy different parts of the district. Any predictor that varies smoothly in space can then act as a proxy for location. Blocking at 10 km cut the location-only AUC to {f2(g('Block 10km','Coordinates only','RF (default)')[0])} but not to 0.5, so a residual geographic signal remains in the reported scores. Drawing non-landslide samples by a random or stratified design over the whole district, rather than placing them by eye, would reduce this bias in future inventories [@hussin2016; @steger2016b].")
if PAS is not None:
    P(f"The random-sample test (Section 4.10) puts a number on this. With non-landslide points spread over the whole district, the location-only signal disappeared and the ensemble AUC dropped by about {_po.AUC_ensemble - _pr.AUC_ensemble.mean():.2f}. We read the reported nested AUC of {f3(m('Ensemble-equal','cal','AUC'))} as an upper bound that holds for the supplied sampling design, and the random-sample AUCs as a conservative lower bound. The conclusions that do not depend on the design are the ones we emphasize: terrain form, aspect and distance to roads control susceptibility in both designs, soil texture and monsoon rainfall carry little independent information at the resolution of their products, and the relative ranking of the district is broadly stable.")
P("The fold-to-fold range of AUC (Table 5) was ten times wider than the repeat-to-repeat standard deviation. Performance depends on which region is held out, and a single test split, random or spatial, cannot capture this. Reporting the spread across spatial folds is therefore as important as reporting the mean.")
H2('5.2. Model Choice, Ensembles and Calibration')
P(f"The four algorithms performed almost identically on held-out regions, and none was consistently best after correction for multiple testing. This agrees with comparative work showing that no algorithm dominates across study areas [@goetz2015; @pourghasemi2018] and contradicts the clear XGBoost advantage reported earlier, which came from a random split that had already been used to choose the predictor set. The equal-weight ensemble was the most dependable choice: it gave the highest AUC in every repeat, although the margin was significant after Holm correction in all three repeats only against {' and '.join(beat) if beat else 'none of the single models'}, consistent with the error-averaging argument for diverse learners [@dietterich2000]. Picking a winner on inner folds, by contrast, cost {f3(m('Ensemble-equal','cal','AUC')-m('Best single (inner-CV)','cal','AUC'))} in AUC because the inner-loop winner changed from fold to fold. Weighting and stacking added nothing measurable, so the equal-weight ensemble is the simplest defensible choice.")
P(f"Calibration mattered more than algorithm choice for the maps. The SVM's very different share of \"susceptible\" pixels in the original submission (34.6% at a 0.5 threshold, against 2.8–7.1% for the tree models) is consistent with its raw calibration slope of {f2(m('SVM','raw','CalSlope'))} here, but RF ({f2(m('RF','raw','CalSlope'))}) was similarly under-confident and GBM ({f2(m('GBM','raw','CalSlope'))}) and XGBoost ({f2(m('XGBoost','raw','CalSlope'))}) were slightly over-confident, so the divergence is a property of all four score scales rather than of the SVM alone. Averaging raw scores from models with different score scales mixes these errors. Calibrating each model on inner folds before averaging removes most of the problem. Calibration targets the balanced sample, however, so calibrated scores remain relative: converting them to absolute probabilities would require a prior-correction based on a defensible estimate of landslide prevalence, which an event inventory of this kind does not provide [@vancalster2019].")
H2('5.3. Agreement and Dispersion as Reliability Evidence')
P(f"The original submission described the 2.47% of pixels with four susceptible votes as the only area of model consensus. That is incorrect: zero votes is also unanimous agreement, so binary unanimity covered 65.19% + 2.47% = 67.66% of the district in that analysis. More importantly, agreement among algorithms trained on the same data is not evidence of correctness. On held-out samples, {AT.loc[0,'landslide_share']*100:.0f}% of unanimous non-susceptible samples were landslides. Agreement and dispersion are useful because their relation to error is now measured: error rates were {AT.loc[[1,2,3],'ensemble_error'].min()*100:.0f}–{AT.loc[[1,2,3],'ensemble_error'].max()*100:.0f}% where votes split and rose roughly {ZT.misclass.iloc[3]/max(ZT.misclass.iloc[0],1e-3):.0f}-fold from the lowest to the highest dispersion quartile. We therefore present them as inter-model disagreement layers that flag where predictions are less reliable, not as uncertainty quantification. They miss shared sources of error, among them inventory gaps, pseudo-absence design, positional error and predictor errors [@steger2016a].")
H2('5.4. Training–Raster Consistency and Spectral Leakage')
P("Two of the most consequential findings of this revision came from auditing data rather than tuning models. The parent-soil predictor, previously excluded for a legend mismatch, had been read from the wrong column; the correct column shares the raster legend and the predictor could be restored. More serious are the differences in roughness, TRI, TPI and TWI between the training table and the rasters (Table 4), and the shift in MBI shown by district-wide statistics. All Model T results reported here were therefore refit on raster-sampled values. The audit also exposed a duplicated layer: the supplied curvature raster is TPI divided by −100, so including both would have counted one variable twice, inflated its share of importance and distorted distance-based learners such as the SVM. A model trained on one version of a variable and applied to another will produce maps whose errors are invisible in cross-validation, because cross-validation never sees the raster. Sampling every predictor from the final aligned stack at the inventory coordinates, and checking the result against the stored table, should be a routine step in raster-based susceptibility mapping. The AOA [@meyer2021] then shows where the model extrapolates even when the data are consistent.")
P("The spectral indices pose a different problem. When imagery post-dates the inventory, a vegetation or bare-soil index can recognize existing landslides, and spatial cross-validation will not detect this because the scars are present in the test regions too. Here the imagery dates from September 2024 and overlaps the event, and the very large importance of the spectral family in Model S, together with the direction of its effect, fits this explanation. We therefore report Model T as the susceptibility model and Model S as a leakage diagnostic. Pre-event composites, or masking of mapped scars before index extraction, would allow vegetation and bare-soil information to be used safely.")
H2('5.5. Implications for Susceptibility Screening in Kavrepalanchowk')
P("The product of this study is a relative landslide-susceptibility screening layer for the valid analysis domain of Kavrepalanchowk District. It ranks terrain by similarity to places that failed during the September 2024 rainstorm. It does not estimate when failures will occur, how large they will be, how far they will travel or what they will damage, and it should not be read as a hazard or risk map [@fell2008]. Used with the dispersion, agreement and applicability layers, it can help district authorities decide where field verification and geotechnical assessment should start. Transfer to other districts or to earthquake-triggered failures has not been tested.")
H2('5.6. Limitations')
P("Some limitations were addressed in this revision; others require data that do not yet exist. Addressed: geographic sampling bias was quantified with a coordinates-only baseline; the validation block size was chosen from a correlogram and a sensitivity test; all modelling decisions were nested; calibration was measured and corrected; ensemble weights were learned only from training folds; collinearity was re-evaluated with correct VIFs and a duplicated terrain layer was removed; importance was estimated by permutation on held-out regions; and sensitivity to the non-landslide sample was tested with five random realizations.")
P("Requiring new data: the inventory covers a single rainstorm (26–30 September 2024) and rainfall-triggered failures only, so performance for other seasons or for earthquake-triggered landslides is unknown; the non-landslide points were placed manually rather than by a random design, so the sensitivity test (Section 4.10) can bound but not remove the effect of their placement; rainfall and soil products are much coarser than the 30-m grid, so their 30-m values are interpolated rather than observed; and the landslide points are not linked to polygons, so landslide size and positional error could not be modelled. Independent validation with a later inventory, ideally from another district, is the most important next step.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6 Conclusions
H1('6. Conclusions')
P(f"We re-evaluated a machine-learning landslide susceptibility workflow for Kavrepalanchowk District, Nepal, under nested spatial cross-validation and a training–raster audit. With {TSRP} predictors, four classifiers reached out-of-fold AUCs of {f3(min(m(k,'cal','AUC') for k in ['RF','XGBoost','GBM','SVM']))}–{f3(max(m(k,'cal','AUC') for k in ['RF','XGBoost','GBM','SVM']))} on held-out 10-km regions, and an equal-weight ensemble of calibrated scores reached {f3(m('Ensemble-equal','cal','AUC'))} with a Brier score of {f3(m('Ensemble-equal','cal','Brier'))}. No single algorithm was reliably better than another, the equal-weight ensemble was the safest choice, and raw scores differed widely in calibration, so inner-fold Platt calibration was needed before averaging them. A coordinates-only model showed that the inventory is strongly structured in space, which random validation rewards; with random non-landslide points the ensemble AUC fell to about {_pr.AUC_ensemble.mean():.2f}, but the terrain and road effects held. Spectral indices from imagery overlapping the event raised accuracy but behaved like a record of the landslides themselves; they should be used only from imagery acquired before the triggering season. The audit found a column mix-up in the soil predictor, VIFs computed without an intercept, a duplicated terrain layer (curvature = −TPI/100) and distribution shifts between the training table and several rasters. The four models agreed unanimously on {(AT.loc[0,'n']+AT.loc[4,'n'])/V['n']*100:.0f}% of held-out samples, yet about one in five of those was misclassified; split votes and high dispersion marked where errors concentrated. After all predictors were re-sampled from the mapping rasters, the district map passed a class-wise test with held-out landslides: the Very High quintile held {pd.read_csv('out_class_validation.csv').share_pct.iloc[4]:.0f}% of them and the upper two quintiles {pd.read_csv('out_class_validation.csv').share_pct.iloc[3:].sum():.0f}%, and {json.load(open('out_raster_summary.json'))['aoa_inside_pct']:.0f}% of the district lay inside the area of applicability. The resulting susceptibility score, with its dispersion, agreement and applicability layers, is suited to screening and prioritizing field checks within the valid analysis domain.")

